 "comment": "A firm with three partners and one s.194H payee: 5 rows at one rate each, 4 findings. The payee's row comes first (p08, Ivy Agency, Rs 20,000.01 at 2%), then the partners' rows at 10%, in the order of their keys (juniper, kapok, laurel), which is not the order the partners table lists them in. Juniper, classified one-off by its key in the partners table: p01 (interest, Rs 15,000) is within the s.194T limit; p02 (salary, Rs 12,000) takes the total over it on 31 December, so p01 and p02 are one tranche dated 31 December; p03 (salary, Rs 9,000) is its own tranche on 31 March. Kapok, not classified: p04 credits interest of Rs 15,000 net of Rs 1,500 on TDS on Partners, a ledger classified as TDS payable, and is read gross; p05 debits the capital Rs 8,000 against the interest ledger, a reversal that lowers nothing and is in no tranche; p06 (salary, Rs 6,000) takes the gross total to Rs 21,000 on 15 January, so p04 and p06 are one tranche dated 15 January. Read net of the reversal, or net of the TDS, the total would stay within the limit. Laurel, classified foreseeable by its key: p07 (interest, Rs 20,000.01) is one tranche on 30 June.",
